          "title": "You'll want to know: einloggen",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "einloggen — \"to log in (reflexive, sich einloggen)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ohne Passwort kann ich mich nicht einloggen. I cannot log in without a password."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Reflexive and separable: ich logge mich ein, ich habe mich eingeloggt. Also sich anmelden."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "einloggen takes English log in and gives it the German prefix ein- \"in\". English log in is usually traced to the ship's log, the daily book named after the wooden log once used to measure a ship's speed; EtymDB has no entry for the German verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
